Files:
product/
crates/tools/
crates/release/
Do:
Use the playable slice, replay evidence, and measured performance regime to define only the production concerns that concretely bind.
Give each activated concern its own contract and owner for content tooling, packaging, audio/input integration, synchronization, release targets, or other platform work.
Keep deferred concerns visible as gates rather than allowing them to reshape the kernel, worldline model, or renderer implicitly.
Accept:
There is a reviewed product scope with explicit target platforms, packaging, content workflow, and integration boundaries for every concern that was activated.
Each activated platform feature has a contract, dependency, owner, and acceptance evidence; deferred networking, merge, large-scale authoring, and final GPU choices remain explicitly deferred.
The release path can reproduce the canonical trace and measured target regime without weakening the semantic or deterministic contracts.
> Gate: Choose production targets, packaging and distribution, content tooling, and audio/input integration only when the slice and measurements show they bind.
> Gate: Decide whether networking, synchronization, or merge semantics are required; no architecture is presumed here.
> Gate: Decide final renderer and GPU architecture from the measured workload, preserving the GameState plus Tau boundary.
